WEBVTT

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:03.220
Here is a realistic, disciplined trading plan.

00:00:03.770 --> 00:00:09.880
A $100,000 balance. Three trades a day, three days a week. One percent risk on each.

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:15.190
Each trade risks $1,000. That is the stop loss.

00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:20.900
The target is 3 percent: $3,000. A risk-to-reward of one to three.

00:00:21.450 --> 00:00:25.730
And once a trade moves into profit, the stop loss moves to break-even.

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:32.510
Watch one trade. Entry here. Stop loss below. Target three times further above.

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:37.930
The price moves up, into profit. Now the stop loss moves up to the entry price.

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:43.050
From here, the trade cannot lose. If the price turns back, it closes at zero.

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:47.280
If it keeps going, it hits the target: plus $3,000.

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:53.340
A realistic day. One trade hits the target: plus $3,000.

00:00:53.890 --> 00:00:56.220
One returns to break-even: zero.

00:00:56.770 --> 00:00:59.570
One hits the stop loss: minus $1,000.

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:02.880
The day: plus $2,000. Two percent.

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:08.120
At one to three, you break even winning just one trade in four.

00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:14.010
Even the worst day, three losses, costs $3,000. Three percent of the account.

00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:18.650
Three days a week is about 13 trading days a month.

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:23.180
If the average day matched the example: $26,000 a month,

00:01:23.730 --> 00:01:27.170
and about $256,000 after six months.

00:01:28.870 --> 00:01:31.480
Now withdraw 3 percent of the balance every month.

00:01:32.030 --> 00:01:37.490
Your initial $100,000 is fully withdrawn by month 14, while the account keeps growing.

00:01:40.870 --> 00:01:44.090
These are projections from one example day, not results.

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:53.680
Trading CFDs and FX contracts is highly speculative and involves significant risk to your capital. Never risk more than you can afford.
